# November 18, 2026: Orleans County, New York (Albion, Medina, Holley, Childs, Clarendon, Waterport), the first pass
# there. Drawn at random by tools/choose.py (region wny, which offered Holley, Clarendon and Jamestown); the Atlas had
# nothing within 25 km of Albion. Mostly the Erie Canal and the Medina sandstone and cobblestone buildings that stand
# along it, with the Waterport bridge ruins and the old Holley canal loop for ruins. Sources opened in this session:
# Wikipedia as raw text (tools/wiki_raw.py), the HAER record for Culvert Road (PDF, text layer read), and the Orleans
# Hub, Orleans County Tourism, Cobblestone Museum, Medina Railroad Museum and gowaterfalling.com pages through
# tools/page.py.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'wny_orln','label':'Orleans County: the Erie Canal, Medina sandstone and cobblestones','added':'2026-11-18 21:22'}
REG=L('Wikipedia: National Register listings in Orleans County, New York','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Register_of_Historic_Places_listings_in_Orleans_County,_New_York')
PLACES=[
N('culvert-road-medina','Culvert Road, the road under the Erie Canal','Ridgeway','wny','canal','standing',43.235286,-78.340411,'exact','public','drive','canal','1823',
  'The one place on the whole canal system where a road passes under the Erie Canal: here the canal runs on a high berm and Culvert Road goes through it in a stone culvert. A sign on the headwall says the culvert was built in 1823; the Canal Corporation\'s panel (as the Orleans Hub reported it in 2015) says the first culvert was taken apart in 1854, rebuilt the next year and upgraded again in 1895. Ripley\'s Believe It or Not featured it.',
  'A one-lane stone tunnel with arched cut-stone headwalls, about 7 ft 6 in of clearance and concrete sidewalks along both sides (HAER, 2009, which found it in fair condition with a lot of water leaking through). The interpretive panel is on the towpath on the north side of the culvert.',
  'Drive under the Erie Canal.',
  'A public town road off NY 31 northwest of the Telegraph Road junction, Town of Ridgeway; the canal and its towpath (Erie Canalway Trail) above are New York State Canal Corporation land. One lane: watch for oncoming cars and for people on the sidewalks. No lot is mapped; the towpath is reached from the road.',
  [L('HAER NY-495, New York State Barge Canal, Culvert Road (PDF, via historicbridges.org)','https://historicbridges.org/newyork/culvertroad/ny2302data.pdf'),L('Orleans Hub, July 14, 2015: Medina celebrates panel by Canal Culvert','https://orleanshub.com/?p=12095')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('medina-aqueduct-falls','Medina Aqueduct and Medina Falls','Medina','wny','canal','standing',43.22153,-78.38305,'site','public','walk','canal','1849 or 1913–1918',
  'At Medina the Erie Canal crosses the gorge of Oak Orchard Creek, the canal carried over the river with Medina Falls just downstream. Sources differ on what stands: Orleans County Tourism describes a stone aqueduct of Medina sandstone built in 1849, while the 2023 Orleans Hub article on the new historic marker describes the Barge Canal crossing of 1913–1918, a concrete structure with architectural adornment and a concrete retaining wall that is in fact a dam, and calls it the only true aqueduct left on the Erie Canal.',
  'From the towpath on the north side you look down about 40 ft to the falls, which spread over sandstone ledges, and across the aqueduct with the river passing beneath the canal. A historic marker about the aqueduct stands in the canal basin (installed 2023). Steep informal paths go down into the gorge; they are not a managed trail.',
  'A river crossing under a canal.',
  'The towpath (Erie Canalway Trail) is New York State Canal Corporation land. The tourism page gives Laurel Street as the address. gowaterfalling.com (2009) directs you east on Center Street (NY 31), left on State Street, then left over the one-lane canal bridge to a small trailhead lot, and west a few hundred feet on the towpath. Hours not checked.',
  [L('Orleans County Tourism: Medina Falls & Aqueduct','https://www.orleanscountytourism.com/destinations/medina-falls-and-aqueduct'),L('Orleans Hub, July 16, 2023: new historic marker celebrates Medina Aqueduct','https://orleanshub.com/?p=187157'),L('gowaterfalling.com: Medina Falls','https://gowaterfalling.com/waterfalls/medina.shtml')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('medina-main-street','Medina Main Street Historic District','Medina','wny','town','standing',43.220278,-78.386944,'area','view','walk','canal','1830s–1920s',
  'Medina grew at a bend in the Erie Canal that made a natural harbour, and its Main Street runs a block and a half from the canal south to the railroad. The brick and stone commercial buildings date from the 1830s to the 1920s, mostly Italianate; the finest is Bent\'s Opera House at Main and Center, and the old main station at 615 Main is one of those built of the local Medina sandstone. The district was listed on the National Register in 1995, and the freight depot (now the railroad museum) was added in 1997.',
  'A walkable canal-town main street of brick and red sandstone fronts, from the canal basin south to the tracks; Bent\'s Opera House stands at Main and Center. Many buildings were renovated after a mid-century decline. Most are working shops; interiors not checked.',
  'A canal-bend harbour that became a sandstone town.',
  'Public streets in the Village of Medina; view the buildings from the sidewalk. A mapped public lot is on Mill Street by the canal basin (OpenStreetMap).',
  [W('Main Street Historic District (Medina, New York)'),REG],th=[43.22123,-78.38621],thn='Mill Street lot by the canal basin',brick='some'),
N('medina-railroad-museum','Medina freight depot (Medina Railroad Museum)','Medina','wny','rail','standing',43.21832,-78.39028,'exact','open','drive','stations','1905',
  'A New York Central freight depot of 1905, one of the largest wooden freight depots still standing. It is now the Medina Railroad Museum, with about 6,000 artifacts, a model railroad and old firefighting equipment, and it runs excursion trains between Medina and Lockport. It became part of the Main Street Historic District in 1997.',
  'The long wooden freight house itself is the thing to see, with the museum inside. Which original fittings survive inside was not checked.',
  'One of the biggest wooden freight houses left.',
  'Run by the Medina Railroad Museum, 530 West Avenue. Open Thursday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. and Sunday noon to 4:30 p.m., closed Monday to Wednesday; admission charged (museum home page, read November 18, 2026). Train rides are ticketed separately.',
  [L('Medina Railroad Museum','https://medinarailroadmuseum.org/'),W('Medina Railroad Museum')],brick='no'),
N('medina-armory','Medina Armory','Medina','wny','civic','standing',43.221944,-78.392222,'exact','view','walk','','1901',
  'A two-storey armory of Medina sandstone laid in random ashlar, with an administration building on Prospect Avenue and a drill shed behind. It was the first armory designed by state architect George Heins, for the 29th Separate Company of the National Guard. The Guard left in 1977 and it is now the local YMCA; it was listed on the National Register in 1995.',
  'The red sandstone front with its projecting entrance pavilion faces south across a lawn. Inside it is a working YMCA; interior not checked.',
  'A state armory built of the town\'s own stone.',
  'Owned and used by the YMCA (Lake Plains YMCA, per the Register list). View from Pearl Street or Prospect Avenue (NY 63); hours not checked.',
  [W('Medina Armory'),REG],brick='no'),
N('waterport-bridge-ruins','Waterport trestle piers and old bridge walls','Carlton','wny','rail','ruins',43.32776,-78.23787,'area','view','hike','','1850s?–1990s',
  'Just below the Waterport Dam on the Oak Orchard River. The railroad trestle that crossed here was taken down about 1993 (the Orleans Hub said in 2013 it had been gone 20 years), but its massive stone piers and bases remain. North of it are the sandstone walls of two older road bridges: one, perhaps of the 1850s, keeps a wall, a wooden beam and some cast-iron pins; the other, of the late 1800s or early 1900s, carried Park Avenue to Clark Mills Road and has walls about 12 ft high and steel girders in the woods. The dates are a local man\'s estimates, not a document\'s.',
  'The trestle piers stand by the river where fishermen pass. The older bridge walls are on the west bank north of the trestle, off any worn path; the farther one is hidden in the woods.',
  'Stone piers of three crossings, in a salmon run.',
  'The east-bank Oak Orchard River Fishing Access Area (about 16 acres) is leased by Orleans County for public fishing and boating; use the DEC trail from Park Avenue Extension at the north end or the utility and DEC parking area at the south end, not Park Avenue itself; carry in, carry out; no camping or fires (county rules sheet). The land round the dam belongs to Brookfield Power, and who owns the west bank where the older walls are was not checked: treat that side as ask-first. The pin is where OpenStreetMap maps the old Ontario Branch crossing the river.',
  [L('Orleans Hub, Oct. 23, 2013: In Waterport wilderness, signs from a different era','https://orleanshub.com/?p=17286'),L('Orleans County: Oak Orchard River Fishing Access Area rules (PDF)','https://www.orleanscountytourism.com/storage/files/5408177/OOR-Fishing-Access-Area-Rules.pdf')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('cobblestone-inn-oak-orchard','Cobblestone Inn','Oak Orchard-on-the-Ridge','wny','hotel','standing',43.274444,-78.333056,'exact','view','drive','hotels','1830s',
  'A stagecoach inn of the 1830s on Ridge Road (NY 104), the old route along the Lake Ontario shore, believed to be the largest cobblestone building in the state. It stayed an inn into the mid-1900s after the stagecoaches gave way to railroads, then was a guest house, restaurant and antique shop until 2010, and is now a residence. It was listed on the National Register in 2007.',
  'A two-storey L-shaped building faced in cobblestones, five rows to each Medina sandstone quoin, under a hipped roof with one central chimney. Samples of its wallpapers are kept at the Cobblestone Museum in Childs. Interior not checked.',
  'Perhaps the biggest cobblestone building anywhere.',
  'A private home at the northwest corner of NY 104 and Oak Orchard River Road (County Route 53): view from the road only.',
  [W('Cobblestone Inn'),REG],brick='?'),
N('cobblestone-museum-childs','Cobblestone Historic District (Cobblestone Museum)','Childs','wny','landmark','standing',43.28694,-78.19083,'site','open','drive','','1834–1849',
  'Three cobblestone buildings on Ridge Road at Childs make the smallest National Historic Landmark district in the state (1993): the First Universalist Church of 1834, the oldest cobblestone church in North America, its parsonage (the Ward House, 1836) and District School No. 5 of 1849. The church had been abandoned and used to store cabbages when the Cobblestone Society bought it about 1960 to save it; the society restored the buildings and runs them as a museum, and rebuilt the church tower in 1964.',
  'The church\'s stones are laid in the "Gaines pattern" of hexagonal mortar joints, with brick lintels and the inscription "Erected by the First Universalist Society AD 1834 God is Love"; inside it is arranged as in the 1880s. The school is a wood frame with a cobblestone veneer only about 10 in thick, a rare method, and is largely as it was when last used in the 1950s. The tour also takes in the museum\'s other buildings (Farmers Hall, print, harness and blacksmith shops).',
  'The oldest cobblestone church on the continent, once a cabbage store.',
  'Owned by the Cobblestone Society & Museum, Ridge Road (NY 104) just east of NY 98. Guided tours with admission ($9 adults, children free; allow 2 hours); "shoulder season" from October 1, 2026, call (585) 589-9013 for tour times, and off-season tours by appointment only (museum page, read November 18, 2026). All buildings have steps except the church\'s lower level. A mapped lot is on Oak Orchard Road just northwest.',
  [L('Cobblestone Museum: Admission & Hours','https://www.cobblestonemuseum.org/admission-hours/'),L('Orleans Hub, Sept. 26, 2020: the Cobblestone Universalist Church','https://orleanshub.com/?p=131587'),L('Orleans Hub, Oct. 10, 2020: Childs schoolhouse','https://orleanshub.com/?p=132372'),W('Cobblestone Historic District')],th=[43.28749,-78.19215],thn='Lot on Oak Orchard Road',brick='?'),
N('gaines-cobblestone-schoolhouse','Gaines District No. 2 Cobblestone Schoolhouse','Gaines Basin','wny','civic','standing',43.2571,-78.22369,'exact','view','drive','','1832',
  'A one-room school built of local cobblestone in 1832 for the families who came with the Erie Canal, across the road from the log school it replaced. It taught children until 1944, when the district joined Albion Central, and then stood empty for decades. Restoration began in 2014 under the Orleans County Historical Association, which was given the building; it was listed on the National Register in 2017, and by 2021 the interior had been restored to its 1940s look with the original teacher\'s desk.',
  'A small cobblestone schoolhouse with a historical marker (2015) and a donated outhouse moved there in 2017.',
  'A canal-era school brought back to its last day of class.',
  'Owned by the Orleans County Historical Association, which plans history programs there; open days and hours not checked. View from the road.',
  [W('Gaines District No. 2 Cobblestone Schoolhouse'),REG],brick='no'),
N('orleans-county-courthouse-square','Orleans County Courthouse Square, Albion','Albion','wny','civic','standing',43.24586,-78.1928,'site','view','walk','','1830s–1930s',
  'The civic centre of Albion, the county seat. William V. N. Barlow\'s Greek Revival courthouse with its metal dome (begun in the late 1850s, modelled on the Wayne County courthouse at Lyons) stands among churches of seven denominations, the Swan Library and the 1937 post office. The spire of the 1874 First Presbyterian Church, about 175 ft, is the tallest structure in the county. The district was listed on the National Register in 1979.',
  'The domed courthouse in its tree-planted square, the Medina sandstone used on many of the buildings round it, and the Presbyterian spire to the north. The Pullman church is a separate entry. The courthouse is a working court; interior not checked.',
  'A county seat built round its dome and its spires.',
  'Public streets in the Village of Albion (South Main / NY 98, East State, Platt and Park streets); view from the sidewalks.',
  [W('Orleans County Courthouse Historic District'),L('Orleans Hub, May 16, 2015: two Albion churches on the Sacred Sites tour','https://orleanshub.com/?p=21439'),REG],brick='?'),
N('pullman-memorial-church','Pullman Memorial Universalist Church','Albion','wny','worship','standing',43.24499,-78.19352,'exact','view','walk','','1894',
  'Built in 1894 and dedicated in 1895 with money from George Pullman, the sleeping-car maker who grew up in Albion, in memory of his parents. Solon S. Beman designed it in pink Medina sandstone in a Richardsonian manner with a crossing tower. It has been in use ever since; the congregation joined the Unitarian Universalist Association in 1961.',
  'Original fabric: Tiffany Glass and Decorating Company windows (Wikipedia gives fifty-six windows in its opening and forty-one in the sanctuary later on; the Orleans Hub says 41), including "Christ the Consoler", which Tiffany featured in an 1898 brochure; quarter-sawn golden-oak woodwork; oak plank doors with wrought-iron hinges; a Johnson pipe organ with stencilled gold-leaf pipes.',
  'Pullman\'s church and its Tiffany glass.',
  'An active church at 10 East Park Street. It has opened for public tours on the Sacred Sites weekend (9 a.m. to 3 p.m. in May 2015, per the Orleans Hub); other visiting times not checked. Otherwise view from the street.',
  [W('Pullman Memorial Universalist Church'),L('Orleans Hub, May 16, 2015: two Albion churches on the Sacred Sites tour','https://orleanshub.com/?p=21439')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('mount-albion-cemetery','Mount Albion Cemetery and the Soldiers and Sailors tower','Albion','wny','burial','standing',43.23963,-78.15488,'exact','public','drive','cemeteries','1840s',
  'A rural cemetery laid out in the 1840s on a glacial drumlin east of Albion and owned by the village. On its highest point stands the Soldiers and Sailors Monument, a round Medina sandstone tower built from 1874 and dedicated on July 4, 1876, to the county\'s Civil War dead; their names are on marble tablets inside (the tourism pages give 463 and 466). The Gothic sandstone chapel dates from 1875, the pond from 1908 and the Ingersoll fountain from 1914. It was the first place in the county listed on the National Register (1976).',
  'A carved sandstone entrance arch with a slate roof and iron gates, the chapel just inside, and the 68-ft tower on the hill: a door on its north side opens on a spiral stair of 84 steps, much of it dark and tight, to a lookout over the country. A domed spring house dated 1908 is on its south side.',
  'Climb a Civil War memorial tower in a cemetery.',
  'Owned and run by the Village of Albion, on NY 31 east of the village; cemetery office (585) 589-4174. Visiting hours, and when the tower is open, not checked. A small lot is by the chapel inside the gate (Wikipedia).',
  [W('Mount Albion Cemetery'),L('Orleans County Tourism: Mount Albion Cemetery','https://www.orleanscountytourism.com/destinations/mount-albion-cemetery'),L('Orleans County Tourism: Soldiers & Sailors Monument','https://www.orleanscountytourism.com/destinations/soldiers-and-sailors-monument')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('albion-north-main-bank','Albion canal front (North Main–Bank Streets Historic District)','Albion','wny','town','standing',43.2475,-78.193611,'area','view','walk','canal','1830s–1940s',
  'Albion\'s commercial core grew up as a stop on the Erie Canal, and after three fires in the 1800s burned the wooden buildings it was rebuilt in Medina sandstone and brick. It is one of the most intact commercial streets along the canal; it includes an opera house, the village hall and a stretch of the canal itself with two of its bridges and the old towpath. Listed on the National Register in 1994.',
  'North Main Street down to the canal bridge, the canal and towpath east to the Ingersoll Street bridge, and the Burrows Block at 131½ North Main, the one survivor of a row that once faced the canal. Interiors not checked.',
  'A canal street rebuilt in stone after three fires.',
  'Public streets and the canal towpath (Canal Corporation) in the Village of Albion; a village parking lot is behind East Bank Street.',
  [W('North Main–Bank Streets Historic District'),REG],brick='some'),
N('holley-canal-falls','Holley Canal Falls','Holley','wny','canal','traces',43.224942,-78.018948,'exact','public','walk','canal','1800s',
  'A man-made waterfall: a waste weir lets surplus water out of the Erie Canal into the East Branch of Sandy Creek, which drops about 40 ft into a gorge. When it was first made is not known to the source that describes it. The gorge is a ravine so deep that the first canal builders swung the canal in a loop to cross it at a narrow point in 1823, and the state straightened it over a high embankment in 1854–61; the Orleans Hub says some of that original loop bed survives east of the Public Square.',
  'The falls from the small park at their foot, with picnic tables and a shelter; visible from the road and the parking area.',
  'A waterfall made by a canal.',
  'Canal Park, Village of Holley. From the southeast corner of the Public Square take Frisbee Terrace to its end (gowaterfalling.com, 2009); OpenStreetMap maps the park there. Hours not checked.',
  [L('gowaterfalling.com: Holley Canal Falls','https://gowaterfalling.com/waterfalls/holleycanal.shtml'),L('Orleans Hub, Oct. 14, 2015: original canal loop in Holley','https://orleanshub.com/?p=12340')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('clarendon-stone-store','Clarendon Stone Store (Old Stone Store)','Clarendon','wny','town','standing',43.1938,-78.0647,'exact','view','drive','','1836',
  'A general store built of Medina sandstone in 1836 (Orleans Hub), one of the first buildings anywhere of that stone. David Sturges kept it first and his son-in-law George Copeland ran it from 1843 to 1892; it also held the post office and town court, and stayed a store until 1975. It was then apartments, stood empty from 2007, and was condemned; the town was ready to demolish it in 2011, sold it for one dollar to Sue and Joe Fertitta, who restored it from 2012, and a flooring shop opened on the ground floor in 2016.',
  'The two-storey sandstone store at the crossroads, with a reproduction "Buffalo Paints" sign naming George H. Copeland over the door (painted by Carol Culhane from an 1880s photograph, put up in 2015). Ground floor commercial, apartment above; interior not checked.',
  'Saved from the wrecking ball for a dollar.',
  'Privately owned, at NY 31A (East Lee Road) and NY 237 in the hamlet of Clarendon. Whether a business is open there now was not checked; view from the road.',
  [W('Clarendon Stone Store'),L('Orleans Hub, Feb. 11, 2016: Historic Stone Store welcomes new tenant','https://orleanshub.com/?p=11340'),L('Orleans Hub, Sept. 22, 2015: replica sign added','https://orleanshub.com/?p=12488')],brick='no'),
N('hillside-cemetery-clarendon','Hillside Cemetery','Clarendon','wny','burial','standing',43.21628,-78.03053,'site','public','drive','cemeteries','1866',
  'A rural cemetery opened in 1866 on the Holley side of Clarendon, where many of the early settlers are buried. It has a Gothic Revival mortuary chapel of 1894 and a stucco-over-concrete storage building of 1928; the last section was added in 1938. A large cenotaph honours the naturalist and taxidermist Carl Akeley, who was born in Clarendon. Listed on the National Register in 2013.',
  'The 1894 chapel and the Akeley cenotaph. The graves also include the medical researcher Lemuel Diggs and the Titanic survivor Lillian Bentham.',
  'Akeley\'s cenotaph and a chapel for the winter dead.',
  'A cemetery on the south edge of Holley; owner and visiting hours not checked. Park on the cemetery drives.',
  [W('Hillside Cemetery (Clarendon, New York)'),REG],brick='?'),
]
